## Message design standard

Every alert follows a strict 6-section structure. Nothing added, nothing removed.

```
Section 1: HEADLINE (1 line)
  Emoji + Symbol + Signal type + Confidence tier
  🟢 / 🔴 / 🟡 encodes direction instantly

Section 2: TRIGGER (2–3 lines)
  Raw facts that fired the signal. No interpretation.
  The numbers only.

Section 3: CONTEXT (3–5 lines)
  Why the environment supports this signal.
  Only GREEN factors. Never list everything.
  Market → Sector → Stock → Technical → Institutional

Section 4: RISK FLAGS (0–2 lines)
  Only if a genuine concern exists.
  If no flags: skip section entirely.
  Absence of warning is itself a positive signal.

Section 5: ACTION (4–5 lines)
  Entry zone, SL (tighter of structure vs ATR), T1, T2, flat-by time.
  Never more than 2R target without exceptional confluence.

Section 6: SUMMARY (1–2 sentences)
  Plain English. Why this is worth looking at.
  Makes the alert feel like an analyst, not a machine.
```

**Special alert types with different formats:**
- **Pre-event warning** — fires when BUY_RUMOR detected. Warns not to chase.
- **Rating action** — after-hours format with next-day pre-open guidance.
- **Sell-the-news** — fires when SPIKE_AND_FADE detected post-announcement.
- **Capitulation watch** — fires at extreme oversold with stabilisation signs.
- **Intermarket divergence warning** — fires when market forces contradict each other.
- **Morning brief** — 09:00 daily. Full global + domestic setup + day's plan.
- **EOD summary** — 18:00 daily. Day's recap + overnight events to watch.

